usb: musb: sprd: wait for the PHY; phy: sprd-ums9632: say when it is registered

ml10 (2026-10-29): the glue gave up on the PHY at the end of the initcalls, took the PHY's reset lines and the
PHY then failed with EBUSY. The glue now waits for the PHY whenever its node exists.

--- a/drivers/usb/musb/musb_sprd.c	2026-10-29 14:12:01.049398853 +0000
+++ b/drivers/usb/musb/musb_sprd.c	2026-10-29 14:12:01.098072692 +0000
@@ -185,7 +185,13 @@
 	}
 
 	/* a usb_phy, as the vendor PHY drivers are; musb keeps its OTG state in the PHY's otg, so it must have one */
+	/*
+	 * The PHY is waited for as long as it takes: without it the controller never sees a session, and taking the
+	 * resets in its place keeps the PHY driver from probing at all (EBUSY, ml10 2026-10-29).
+	 */
 	glue->xceiv = devm_usb_get_phy_by_phandle(dev, "usb-phy", 0);
+	if (glue->xceiv == ERR_PTR(-EPROBE_DEFER))
+		return dev_err_probe(dev, -EPROBE_DEFER, "waiting for the usb-phy\n");
 	if (IS_ERR(glue->xceiv)) {
 		ret = sprd_musb_skip(dev, PTR_ERR(glue->xceiv), "usb-phy");
 		if (ret)
--- a/drivers/usb/phy/phy-sprd-ums9632.c	2026-10-29 14:12:01.058718679 +0000
+++ b/drivers/usb/phy/phy-sprd-ums9632.c	2026-10-29 14:12:01.098904233 +0000
@@ -317,6 +317,7 @@
 	if (ret)
 		return dev_err_probe(dev, ret, "cannot add the usb_phy\n");
 
+	dev_info(dev, "registered\n");
 	return 0;
 }
 
